# Resource-use safeguards

1. Keep heavy tasks sequential. Do not overlap full tests, builds, document rendering or project scans.
2. Test narrowly first. Run tests for changed code during development; run the full suite serially once before the final commit. Repeat only after relevant changes, failures or unresolved concerns. Use `node --test --test-concurrency=1` for the full suite.
3. Bound every scan. Set limits for directories, files, depth and bytes read; report when results are truncated.
4. Reuse fresh results. Check cache timestamps and completed checks before syncing, scanning or regenerating artifacts. Force refresh only when needed.
5. Prevent duplicate syncs. Before live synchronization, check for an equivalent active run. Use a shared cross-process lock across scheduled, CLI and MCP runs when implemented. The runtime lock is not implemented yet; this rule does not claim otherwise. Implementing it remains a separate change requiring authorization.
6. Restart services once. Batch changes and restart the affected service after local checks pass. Restart again only when a new change requires it.
7. Finish the core outcome first. Complete the agreed release gate before adding adjacent features unless the owner explicitly expands scope. For a review or maintenance task, use that task's agreed outcome.
8. Measure before blaming. If resource use spikes, stop launching additional heavy work and inspect available CPU, memory and process evidence. Label unverified explanations as hypotheses.
